<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rhineland</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Rhineland is the loosely defined region of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> along the middle and lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, with cities such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bonn">Bonn</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koblenz">Koblenz</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dusseldorf">Düsseldorf</a>. Fortified by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, it later held the great ecclesiastical electorates and many small states of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> annexed the left bank in the 1790s, most of it passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in 1815, and it was demilitarised after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> until Hitler reoccupied it in 1936.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rhineland.jpg" alt="Rhineland" width="320" height="380" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: QuartierLatin1968 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rheinprovinz-1830.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
